# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# android/pencarimovie-server — native Kotlin + TDLib server app.
#
# LOCAL-ONLY working copy. The APK is built and signed on this machine and the
# SOURCE IS DELIBERATELY NOT IN THE REPOSITORY — only the published release
# assets are, on the rolling `apk-server-latest` tag. The `android/*` rule above
# therefore excludes this whole tree; do NOT add a `!android/pencarimovie-server/`
# re-include (it was intentionally removed — the repo carries releases + the PHP
# server only).
#
# Cutting a release from this machine:
#   1. cd android/pencarimovie-server && gradlew :app:assembleDebug
#   2. cd .. && powershell -File _stage_release.ps1      (stages + hashes + version.json)
#   3. gh release upload apk-server-latest \
#        _apk_dist/*.apk _apk_dist/*.sha256 _apk_dist/version.json --clobber
# Keep version.json BOM-free: PowerShell's `Set-Content -Encoding UTF8` writes a
# BOM and UpdateChecker's JSONObject() would throw, silently disabling updates.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Runtime state + local build output. NEVER commit anything from storage/:
# it accumulates the Telegram session, the WordPress API secret, the auth
# token and the user's own catalog settings.
storage/*
!storage/.gitkeep
# Android signing material. `release-key.jks` is the identity every installed
# APK trusts, so it must never be published — neither the keystore nor its
# password (read those from local.properties / the environment instead).
*.jks
*.keystore
dist/
dist-native/
_*.txt
_*.log
_pmdeploy.tgz
_heroku*.txt
_apk*.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# legacy/ - superseded implementations.
#
# `legacy/php/` IS tracked: it is the behavioural reference the Rust port is
# verified against (PARITY.md and PORT_GAPS.md at the repo root), so it stays in
# the history. `legacy/ts/` is a dead TypeScript attempt that never reached parity and
# carries a node_modules tree; it is kept on disk for reference only, never
# committed. Same for the port's scratch archives.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
legacy/ts/
# The ledger generator for the archived tree's port audit; the generated
# COVERAGE.md / ledger.json are what we keep.
legacy/php/tools/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# android/ - LOCAL BUILD TREE, deliberately untracked.
#
# Every APK project lives here and none of it belongs in the public repo:
#
#   * it carries the RELEASE SIGNING KEY. `android/**/app/release-key.jks` is
#     the identity every installed APK trusts, so publishing it lets anyone
#     sign an APK that Android accepts as an upgrade over ours — a full
#     supply-chain takeover. The keystore and its password stay on the build
#     machine (`local.properties`, gitignored) — see ANDROID-SIGNING.md.
#   * the compiled engine is a multi-hundred-MB `jniLibs/*.so` blob.
#   * the shipped artifact is the APK, published as a GitHub release asset
#     (`apk-server-latest`), not the source.
#
# Same reasoning already applied to `android/pencarimovie-server` and
# `android/pencarimovie-rust` before this rule existed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
android/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# docs/ - internal design notes, kept out of the published tree.
#
# `docs/` holds working material: the port ledger, stress-run results, the
# Telegram concurrency evaluation and the addon spec. Useful while working on
# the engine, a distraction (and a maintenance liability) in the published
# repository. The QUANTITATIVE record that belongs next to the code —
# PARITY.md, PORT_GAPS.md, COVERAGE.md, CROSS-PLATFORM.md — is tracked at the
# repo root, and README.md is the user-facing document.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/

# The engine's internal engineering bookkeeping. Kept on disk as the working
# record of port parity and platform support; none of it is product
# documentation, and every file here goes stale the moment the code moves.
PARITY.md
PORT_GAPS.md
COVERAGE.md
CROSS-PLATFORM.md

